{"name":"Morocco Red","hex":"#96453b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"morocco","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A soft leather, made from goatskin, used especially in bookbinding.","A sheepskin leather in imitation of this."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/morocco"},{"term":"red","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of the setting sun, blood, and strawberries; the color which is evoked by the longest visible wavelengths (between about 625–740 nm), and a primary additive color.","A revolutionary socialist or (most commonly) a Communist;  a Bolshevik, a supporter of the Bolsheviks in the Russian Civil War."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a red hue.","Having an orange-brown or orange-blond colour; ginger."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red"}],"entries":[{"title":"Morocco","blocks":[{"text":"Morocco, officially the Kingdom of Morocco, is a country in the Maghreb region of North Africa. It has coastlines on the Mediterranean Sea to the north and the Atlantic Ocean to the west, and has land borders with Algeria to the east; the Spanish exclaves of Ceuta, Melilla and Peñón de Vélez de la Gomera along the north, which it claims together with several small Spanish-controlled islands; and the disputed territory of Western Sahara to the south, partly occupied by Morocco since 1975. Morocco also claims to share a border with Mauritania through the disputed territory of Western Sahara. It has a population of approximately 37 million. Its capital is Rabat, while its largest city is Casablanca."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Flag_of_Morocco.svg/330px-Flag_of_Morocco.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morocco","wikidata":"Q1028"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Ida_3_petroglyph.png/330px-Ida_3_petroglyph.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Ida_3_petroglyph.png/330px-Ida_3_petroglyph.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Iron Age petroglyph with Libyco-Berber inscriptions, likely depicting a meteorite fall, found near the High Atlas village of Ida Ou Kazzou","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIda_3_petroglyph.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Volubilis_Longshot_II.jpg/500px-Volubilis_Longshot_II.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Volubilis_Longshot_II.jpg/1280px-Volubilis_Longshot_II.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Volubilis_Longshot_II.jpg/500px-Volubilis_Longshot_II.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Roman ruins of the city of Volubilis, which once served as the capital of Mauretania and the Idrisids","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi